{"benchmark": "ifeval", "task_id": "1000", "sample_idx": 34, "model_name": "mistralai/mixtral-8x7b-instruct-v0.1", "content": " Raymond III Count of Tripoli also known as Raymond of Antioch was a nobleman in the Crusader states of the Levant He was the Count of Tripoli from 1152 until his death in 1187 He was the son of Raymond II Count of Tripoli and Hodierna of Rethel\n\nRaymond III was born around 1140 and succeeded his father as Count of Tripoli in 1152 when he was only twelve years old During his minority the County of Tripoli was ruled by a regent Reynald of Marash who was married to Raymond's mother Hodierna\n\nIn 1156 Raymond III came of age and assumed full control of the County of Tripoli He allied himself with the Byzantine Empire and participated in a failed campaign to retake the city of Shaizar from the Zengid Dynasty\n\nLater in 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus and held prisoner for three years until he was ransomed by the Templars\n\nAfter his release Raymond III resumed his rule over the County of Tripoli and continued to maintain alliances with the Byzantine Empire and the Kingdom of Jerusalem\n\nIn 1174 Raymond III supported the succession of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and played a prominent role in the defense of the Kingdom during the conflict with Saladin the Ayyubid Sultan\n\nRaymond III died on 1187 during the Battle of Hattin at the hands of Saladin's forces His death marked the end of the County of Tripoli as an independent Crusader state\n\n* **Relationship with the Byzantine Empire**\nRaymond III maintained a close relationship with the Byzantine Empire throughout his reign He allied himself with the Byzantines in 1156 and participated in a failed campaign to retake the city of Shaizar from the Zengid Dynasty In 1169 he supported the Byzantine candidate for the throne of the Principality of Antioch\n\n* **Captivity by Nur ad-Din**\nIn 1164 Raymond III was captured by Nur ad-Din the Zengid ruler of Aleppo and Damascus and held prisoner for three years until he was ransomed by the Templars During his captivity Raymond III was held in the castle of Harim and was treated with respect by Nur ad-Din\n\n* **Defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem**\nRaymond III played a prominent role in the defense of the Kingdom of Jerusalem during the conflict with Saladin the Ayyubid Sultan In 1177 he supported the succession of Baldwin IV of Jerusalem and commanded the right flank of the Christian army at the Battle of Montgisard where the Christians emerged victorious over Saladin's forces\n\nIn 1183 Raymond III again commanded the Christian forces in a battle against Saladin near the Springs of Cresson where the Christians were defeated and Raymond III was wounded", "logprobs": [{"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -3.9219088648678735e-05}, {"token": "III", "logprob": -0.0001627074379939586}, {"token": "Count", "logprob": -0.3794739544391632}, {"token": "of", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "Tri", "logprob": -1.4781842764932662e-05}, {"token": "pol", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "i", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "also", "logprob": -0.8585633635520935}, {"token": "known", "logprob": -0.0043882266618311405}, {"token": "as", "logprob": -2.3841855067985307e-07}, {"token": "Raymond", "logprob": -1.168244216387393e-05}, {"token": "of", "logprob": -1.0244133472442627}, {"token": "Ant", "logprob": -0.01800900511443615}, {"token": "io", "logprob": -0.0017049076268449426}, {"token": "ch", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "was", "logprob": -0.004944952670484781}, {"token": "a", "logprob": -0.4789222478866577}, {"token": "noble", "logprob": -0.1473253220319748}, {"token": "man", "logprob": -0.042302753776311874}, {"token": "in", "logprob": -1.9208970069885254}, {"token": "the", "logprob": -8.34461570775602e-06}, {"token": "Cr", "logprob": -0.10340750962495804}, {"token": "us", "logprob": 0.0}, {"token": "ader", "logprob": -1.1920928244535389e-07}, {"token": "states", "logprob": -0.018235063180327415}, {"